$ SLEEP/NET milestone: the `net` standard library module.
$
$ `net` is a BUILT-IN/runtime-backed module, NOT a public Fly library --
$ this is the one deliberate architectural split this milestone draws a
$ hard line on (see the milestone brief §3-§6): SLEEP is real Fly source
$ (sleep.fly, resolved through the public-module/package mechanism);
$ `net` is compiler-known dotted-call builtins bound straight to libflyrt
$ (runtime/net.c), exactly like `filesystem.*`/`path.*` already are (see
$ codegen.cpp's genCall `builtins` table and runtime/net.c). This file
$ exists purely so `bring net` resolves (per docs/architecture.md §3.6's
$ module resolution) and documents the API at the point of use -- same
$ role stdlib/filesystem.fly already plays, see its header comment.
$
$ Every operation below is a BLOCKING plain-TCP-or-TLS primitive and throws
$ a catchable Fly error (via do { ... } grabe (err) { ... }) on failure.
$ TLS/HTTPS follow-up milestone: net.connect_tls() gives a REAL TLS
$ connection (OpenSSL-backed, real certificate + hostname verification --
$ see runtime/net.c and flyrt.h's §5.6 for the implementation). https://
$ origins are reachable by using net.connect_tls() instead of
$ net.connect() and sending the request over it -- see runtime/net.c for
$ the implementation, and dump/public/http.fly for a ready-made HTTP/1.1
$ client built on top of this.
$
$ Available after `bring net`:
$
$   net.resolve(host)          -> tex: first resolved IP address for host
$   net.connect(host, port)    -> num: a connected plain TCP socket handle
$   net.connect_tls(host, port)-> num: a connected, certificate-and-hostname-
$                                  verified TLS connection handle -- looks
$                                  and works just like a plain connect()
$                                  handle to send()/receive()/close() below
$   net.listen(host, port)     -> num: a listening TCP socket handle
$                                  (host "0.0.0.0" binds every interface)
$   net.accept(server)         -> num: blocks for the next client connection
$   net.send(conn, data)       -> num: bytes sent (data is tex, sent as raw bytes; works on a plain or TLS conn)
$   net.receive(conn, maxlen)  -> tex: up to maxlen bytes ("" means the peer closed the connection; works on a plain or TLS conn)
$   net.close(conn)            -> EMP: closes a connect/connect_tls/listen/accept handle
$
$ Example (HTTPS-ish client, manual request -- see dump/public/http.fly
$ for a real client that builds/parses this for you):
$
$   do {
$     conn = net.connect_tls("example.com", 443)
$     net.send(conn, "GET / HTTP/1.1{nl}Host: example.com{nl}Connection: close{nl}{nl}")
$     reply = net.receive(conn, 65536)
$     show(reply)
$     net.close(conn)
$   } grabe (err) {
$     show("net error: {err}")
$   }
$
$ Example (plaintext server, one connection then exit):
$
$   server = net.listen("0.0.0.0", 9000)
$   conn = net.accept(server)
$   msg = net.receive(conn, 1024)
$   net.send(conn, "echo: {msg}")
$   net.close(conn)
$   net.close(server)
